    "updated_at": "2024-04-12T05:49:45Z",
    "body": "I think this might be working fine in the current spec, and what you're seeing is an artifact of your implementation?\r\n\r\n\"scripts may run for the newly-created document\" is only consulted from the call sites on \"loading HTML documents\", \"loading XML documents\", etc. Those state\r\n\r\n> Before any script execution occurs, the user agent must wait for [scripts may run for the newly-created document](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#scripts-may-run-for-the-newly-created-document) to be true for document.\r\n\r\nHowever, these algorithms are never invoked on initial about:blank documents. So for initial about:blank documents, \"scripts may run for a newly-created document\" is completely irrelevant.\r\n\r\nI suspect in your implementation you're using `set_ready_to_run_scripts()` more widely, instead of only using it inside your equivalent of \"load an HTML document\" and friends. So you need to set it in more places. But that isn't how it works in the standard.\r\n\r\nI'll close this since I'm fairly confident in the above. But, if I'm wrong, let me know how, and we'll reopen!",
